<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    AuditLog's cross-section surface, carved into its own leaf project because almost every
    consumer is in Base: ~20 Humans.Application services (Users, Profiles, Camps, Shifts,
    GoogleIntegration, Auth, HumanLifecycle), three Humans.Infrastructure jobs, Shell's
    controllers and seeder, and — the one that settles it — Humans.Application's own
    AuditViewerService. A Contracts/ *folder* inside Humans.AuditLog would therefore make
    Base reference a section and cycle (design §15.5b,
    memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md).

    The surface is deliberately the whole of IAuditLogService: the append path is called
    from ~130 files across every section, and the read members it also carries are the raw
    entry queries the Base-resident AuditViewerService wraps. Notifications' "a wide fan-in
    over a narrow interface is cheap; it is the surface that costs" holds — this is one
    interface and one record.

    References Humans.Interfaces (IApplicationService) and Humans.Domain: AuditAction and
    GoogleSyncSource are Base vocabulary named in these signatures and stay in
    Humans.Domain.Enums, because Shell, Humans.UI's audit view component and ~100 files
    across every section name AuditAction directly (Teams' rule: a Humans.UI renderer is a
    hard floor on how far an enum can move). Campaigns' leaf reference, for the same reason.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Interfaces\Humans.Interfaces.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Domain\Humans.Domain.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
